AI Tools Prompt Pack
Prompts that get real answers — not generic ones
By Elijah

HOW TO USE THIS PACK

Pick a prompt. Replace everything in [brackets] with your own details. Add your context. Then read the answer and say what to fix — the second answer is usually the good one.

The formula behind every good prompt:

1. Context — what is this about, and who are you?
2. Task — exactly what do you want it to do?
3. Format — bullets, steps, under 100 words, a table — say how you want it back.

One rule for all of them: the AI gives you a draft, not a decision. Check anything that matters — prices, addresses, code, facts — before you act on it.

1. LEARN FASTER

1. Explain it simple
Explain [topic] to me like I'm 15 years old. Use one everyday example. No big words unless you explain them first.
Use when: a topic sounds complicated and you want the plain version first.

2. Find my gaps
Ask me 5 questions about [topic], one at a time. After each answer, tell me what I got right, what I missed, and what to study next.
Use when: you want to test yourself instead of just re-reading notes.

3. Check my explanation
I will explain [topic] in my own words. Listen, then point out exactly where my explanation is wrong or incomplete.
Use when: you think you understand something and want proof.

4. Study plan
Make me a 7-day study plan for [topic]. I have [X] minutes a day. Each day: one small lesson and one short exercise.
Use when: you want a plan you can actually follow, not a mountain of material.

5. Summarize it
Summarize [text or topic] in 5 bullet points, then give me the one idea I must remember.
Use when: a long article, lesson, or video needs to become something you can keep.

2. WRITE CLEARLY

6. Rewrite for clarity
Rewrite this so a 15-year-old understands it. Keep all the facts. Cut every unnecessary word: [text]
Use when: your draft is correct but hard to read.

7. Opening lines
Give me 5 opening lines for a post about [topic]. Each one must make the reader want the next sentence. No clickbait, no exaggeration.
Use when: you know what to say but not how to start.

8. Outline first
Before you write anything, give me an outline for [piece] with 3 main points and one example under each.
Use when: you keep getting stuck halfway through writing.

9. Change the tone
Rewrite this in a warm, direct tone, like a friend explaining it. No corporate language: [text]
Use when: your writing sounds stiff or robotic.

10. Cut the fluff
Cut this to half its length. Keep every important point. Show me what you removed and why: [text]
Use when: your message is too long and people stop reading.

3. BUILD AND CODE

11. Explain the error
Explain this error message in plain words, then give me the most likely cause and the fix, step by step: [error]
Use when: an error message reads like a foreign language.

12. Plan before code
Before writing code, break [project] into small steps I can build one at a time. List each step and what "done" looks like.
Use when: a project feels too big to start.

13. Review my code
Review this code like a senior developer. List bugs first, then improvements, then what is already good: [code]
Use when: your code works but you want to know if it is good.

14. Debug with me
Ask me one question at a time to find why [code] is not working. Do not give the answer until we find the cause together.
Use when: you want to understand the bug, not just copy a fix.

15. Learn a tool by building
Teach me [tool] by building one small real thing with it. Explain each step as we go. No theory dump first.
Use when: tutorials bore you and you learn by doing.

4. THINK AND DECIDE

16. Decide between two options
Help me decide between [A] and [B]. List the real tradeoffs, what each choice costs, and what I should check before choosing. Then tell me which one you would pick and why.
Use when: you are stuck between two choices and going in circles.

17. Pros and cons that matter
List the pros and cons of [decision]. Mark which three matter most and why.
Use when: a pros-and-cons list is too long to be useful.

18. Break the goal down
Break [goal] into the smallest first step I can do today, then the next 3 steps. No vague advice — concrete actions only.
Use when: a goal feels big and you keep postponing it.

19. Argue against my plan
Argue against my plan: [plan]. Find the weakest part and tell me what could go wrong.
Use when: you want the holes found before reality finds them.

20. Find the real cause
Ask me "why" 5 times about [problem], one question at a time, until we reach the real cause.
Use when: you keep fixing symptoms and the problem keeps coming back.

5. BUSINESS AND CONTENT

21. Real pain points
List 10 pains people who [struggle with X] actually feel, in their own words. No generic problems — specific, real frustrations.
Use when: you want to sell something people actually need, not something you guess they need.

22. Product description
Write a product description for [product] that says what it does, who it is for, and why it is worth the price — in under 80 words.
Use when: your product page says a lot and sells nothing.

23. Content ideas that teach
Give me 10 post ideas about [topic] that teach something real. Each idea: one line on what the reader learns.
Use when: you want to post but have nothing worth saying.

24. Three angles for one offer
Give me 3 different angles to present [offer]: one about the result, one about the pain it removes, one about the time it saves.
Use when: one offer, one post, and nobody is biting.

25. Respectful reply
Draft a short, respectful reply to [comment or message] that answers the point and adds one useful idea. No begging, no pitch.
Use when: you want to be present in a conversation without selling in it.

6. EVERYDAY AI USE

26. Compare two things
Compare [A] and [B] for [my use]. Tell me which fits better and what I give up with each.
Use when: two tools, two plans, two products — and reviews all say "it depends."

27. Plan the day
Plan my [day or week] around [tasks]. Put the hardest task first, keep breaks, and leave room for things going wrong.
Use when: your plan falls apart by noon every day.

28. Explain a document
Read this and tell me: what is it asking me to do, what is the deadline, and what happens if I ignore it: [document]
Use when: a long message, email, or form hides the one thing you must do.

29. Advice into a checklist
Turn this advice into a checklist I can follow today: [advice]
Use when: good advice stays advice because nobody turned it into steps.

30. Questions to start with
I want to ask an AI about [topic] but I don't know what to ask. Give me the 5 best questions to start with.
Use when: you are new to a topic and don't know where to begin.

THE ONE RULE, AGAIN

Prompts do not make you smart. They make the tool useful. The thinking is still yours: check the answer, fix what is wrong, and keep the part that is right. That is how you learn — one piece at a time.

